--- a/.andromeda/a11y-plan.md
+++ b/.andromeda/a11y-plan.md
@@ -284,3 +284,3 @@
 - **Implementation:** no timeout-extension component is needed, and none is researched or adopted.
-- **CLI:** `viola wait` returns `{timed_out:true}` only for a timeout the caller passes in. The MCP `wait` tool (chunk 2026-10-17-mcp-server-for-drivers) clamps its `timeout_ms` to `WAIT_CAP` 50 000 ms, which is also its default when the caller passes none (`viola_mcp::wait_timeout`), so an MCP `wait` with no timeout is answered `timed-out` after 50 s; that bound falls on the driver only (the hidden, plugin-run `viola mcp` serves a driving Claude session, never a person): no key is blocked, refused or delayed by it, and the wheel is untouched. A raised dialog takes a `viola answer` only within the dialog deadline (`DIALOG_DEADLINE`, 60 s PROVISIONAL, under the `hooks.json` `timeout` of 75 s); on expiry the hook exits 0 with no body, the dialog renders for the human in the `claude` TUI, and a later `answer` for it is refused `not-delivered` / `unknown-dialog` (exit 13) — the deadline bounds the driver, never the human. A `viola send` on a verified CLI waits in the readiness gate for the input box for at most `GATE_MAX_WAIT` (8.5 s, architecture [Screen Model]) and is then refused `not-delivered` / `input-not-ready` with nothing typed. That bound falls on the driver only as well: a key pressed during the wait goes to the child and takes the wheel at once, and the waiting send is then refused `human-typing`. `viola hook statusline` runs the user's own status line command under `STATUSLINE_DEADLINE` (5 s, PROVISIONAL, `src/cmd/hook.rs`) and exits 0 with empty stderr in every case. That bound falls on the user's own command, never on a keystroke and never on the human: no key is blocked, refused or delayed by it, and the wheel is untouched. `viola list` and `viola revive` each make one `claude agents --json` read (a direct spawn, stdout through `take(MAX_FRAME + 1)`, killed at 5 s; chunk 2026-10-16-unwrapped-sessions-on-the-board): a failed, oversize, malformed or timed-out read leaves `list`'s board as `board::read` gave it and passes `revive`'s preflight (fail open). That bound falls on viola's own child only, never on a keystroke or the human: no key is blocked, refused or delayed by it. A budget pause is a bound on the driver only as well (chunk 2026-10-10-budget-governor): while a five-hour or seven-day window stands at or over its threshold (`config.json` `five_hour`, default 90, and `seven_day`, default 85; no value of either switches the gate off) with a known reset still ahead, an automated `viola send` is refused `budget-paused` with detail `five-hour` or `seven-day` (exit 11) and nothing is typed, until that window's reset or a `viola release <name> --budget` for that instance. No key is blocked, refused or delayed by it: a key written while the gate is closed reaches the child, and the outer PTY carries none of viola's own literals (`path6_a_reading_over_the_threshold_refuses_the_send`, `tests/cli_budget.rs`). `answer`, `wait`, `last` and `pause` do not read the gate; that clause is read in the diff (the gate's five product call sites) and, since chunk 2026-10-10-session-links, held by one case: `path6_a_closed_gate_leaves_answer_wait_last_and_pause_alone` (`tests/cli_budget.rs`) closes the gate, reads the automated `send` refused exit 11 `budget-paused` / `five-hour` as its control, and then reads from `wait`, `last`, `answer` and `pause` the exit code and the document each gives under an open gate, with one `send-refused` record in the log (green on the three CI runners, run 38098238731). There is no conformance claim for the terminal.
